color-theme: biotechnology

=== layout: section

# Who buys it, and why?

=== layout: two-panels

# The user and the customer may differ

@left

## User or beneficiary

- Patient receiving a treatment
- Technician running a test
- Community benefiting from cleaner water

@right

## Possible customer

- Hospital or payer
- Testing laboratory or food producer
- Water utility or site owner

@notes

These are possibilities to investigate, not validated customers for a particular proposal.
Ask who approves a purchase and which budget would pay for it.

=== layout: two-panels

# Who pays to find the contamination?

@left

![Food-service photograph used in the summary](assets/business_plan_samples/enterodetect_food.png)

@right

## EnteroDetect sample

- The diner benefits.
- A technician might run the test.
- Who approves the purchase?
- Which budget pays for it?

@notes

Use the actual food-service photograph from the supplied summary as the setting.
Distinguish beneficiary, user, and payer; these are roles to investigate, not validated customers.
The preceding slide retains the patient/hospital, technician/laboratory, and community/utility examples.
Historical student proposals are used to discuss writing and design; their claims are not presented as verified outcomes.

=== layout: two-panels

# Pick one place to start

@right

![Cover bulb artwork and name](assets/business_plan_samples/biolights_logo.png)

@left

## Your first market

- One product. One customer.
- Where does it fit?
- Why would they switch?
- What supports your estimates?
- What comes later?

@notes

BioLights lists several future lighting products; HYGIENIC lists many surfaces. A focused first product is easier to evaluate.
Historical student proposals are used to discuss writing and design; their claims are not presented as verified outcomes.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Excerpt listing possible lighting products](assets/business_plan_samples/biolights_products.png)

@caption

Pick ONE first product. *BioLights excerpt.*

@notes

Ask students to choose between a billboard, porch light, nightlight, or another listed product.
Compare customer, brightness, maintenance, lifetime, and cost. A list of uses is not a commercialization strategy.
Historical student proposals are used to discuss writing and design; their claims are not presented as verified outcomes.

=== layout: two-panels

# Competition includes the current solution

@left

## Direct alternative

Another product using a similar approach for the same customer.

@right

## Indirect alternative

A different way to meet the need, including the customer's current practice.

@notes

BioLights discusses other biotechnology lighting companies but discounts conventional
lighting. Ask which alternatives the customer would actually compare at purchase time.
A different technology or country does not automatically remove competition.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Final paragraph claiming no direct competitors and listing alternatives](assets/business_plan_samples/chronic_competition.png)

@caption

No competitors? Read the list. *Chronic excerpt.*

@notes

Ask: what can the customer already do? A different mechanism can still compete for the same purchase.
Focus on the competition argument, not medical evaluation of the listed treatments.
Historical student proposals are used to discuss writing and design; their claims are not presented as verified outcomes.

=== layout: one-panel

# Compare on the customer's terms

@body

| Option | Performance | Total cost | Practical limit |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Current practice | Cite a result | State the unit | Identify the burden |
| Named competitor | Cite a result | Use the same unit | Explain the tradeoff |
| Your proposal | State a target | Label the estimate | Name the untested step |

@notes

Fill this table with researched information. Empty template cells are prompts, not facts.
Use comparable conditions, dates, units, and customer groups.
Avoid calling a proposed target a measured advantage.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Market paragraph for cost-boundary critique](assets/business_plan_samples/aquazyme_market.png)

@caption

Which cost are we counting? *Aquazyme excerpt.*

@notes

The sample compares ocean-cleanup spending with the cost of genetically modifying a microbe.
Keep the original paragraph visible. Ask what is missing from the cost of delivering and operating a complete service.
The numbers are historical student estimates, not current prices or verified cleanup costs.
Historical student proposals are used to discuss writing and design; their claims are not presented as verified outcomes.

=== layout: two-panels

# Big problem. Big sales?

@left

![Cover logo and tagline](assets/business_plan_samples/aquazyme_logo.png)

@right

## Follow the money

- Who pays for the service?
- What does delivery and operation cost?
- What supports performance at that scale?
- Which costs grow with the amount treated?

@notes

Historical student proposals are used to discuss writing and design; their claims are not presented as verified outcomes.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Financial paragraph; management names above are excluded](assets/business_plan_samples/stem_send_finances.png)

@caption

Sales = profit? Find the costs. *Stem Send excerpt.*

@notes

The sample forecasts one million dollars in sales and also one million dollars in profit while listing costs.
Ask whether timing or categories reconcile these statements. Demand an explicit model rather than assuming a correction.
The next table is a separate, invented classroom scenario; its values do not repair or validate this proposal.
Historical student proposals are used to discuss writing and design; their claims are not presented as verified outcomes.

=== layout: one-panel

# Show the arithmetic and assumptions

@body

| Illustrative monthly scenario (USD) | Calculation | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Revenue | 10 customers x 20 units x 50 | 10,000 |
| Variable production costs | 200 units x 30 | 6,000 |
| Remaining before other costs | 10,000 - 6,000 | 4,000 |
| Other operating costs | Assumed for this exercise | 7,000 |
| Operating result | 4,000 - 7,000 | -3,000 |

@notes

All numbers are invented for classroom arithmetic, not a forecast or investment advice.
Revenue is not profit. This simplified example excludes tax, financing, and capital costs.
The Stem Send sample motivates checking consistency among revenue, expenses, and profit.
Ask what changes if only five customers buy the same quantity.
